  "description": "Seattle-only 160-episode curriculum over 2500, 5000, 7500, and 10000 pedestrians. Population-density-adaptive 8x8 cells are rebuilt from each scenario's exact initial pedestrian-origin mass, with smaller cells where more represented people begin. Stage E uses six four-episode updates to learn natural environment dynamics. Stage A uses twelve updates to learn decision-interval shelter effects. Stage F uses twenty-two joint updates with the dedicated NMCC continuation head trained by backward returns across all eight sequential installations. Exploration, entropy, temperature, and actor/critic learning rates decay independently inside every stage. Every episode starts with two shelters, installs eight equal-capacity shelters at t=15,30,...,120, continues through t=180, and provides exactly 200 places of capacity above the simulated population. NMCC compares each chosen location with the uniform random-site average; no heuristic action prior, action gate, fallback controller, or model-filled candidate is used.",
